<resources>
    <string name="app_name">Trindade</string>

    <!-- Portuguese because the operators are: the app's own copy is the only text here that the
         server did not write, and it is the same language the API answers in. -->
    <string name="login_title">Trindade Massas</string>
    <!-- The web's own tagline for the same screen, from the pt-BR locale's `app.tagline`, so the two
         clients greet the operator with the same words. -->
    <string name="login_tagline">Plataforma Operacional</string>
    <string name="login_username">Usuário</string>
    <string name="login_password">Senha</string>
    <string name="login_submit">Entrar</string>
    <string name="login_unreachable">Sem conexão com o servidor. Verifique a rede e tente de novo.</string>
    <!-- The sentences for the causes that produced no usable answer, which is what UnreachableCause
         enumerates. "Usable" is the precise word, because these are not all about a network:
           - login_timeout covers both OkHttp's connect timeout and its read timeout. The exception type
             cannot tell them apart, so the sentence claims only what the client can know: the answer did
             not arrive in time. It keeps "verifique a rede" out of it, because sending the operator to
             check a network the failure had not been shown to involve is exactly what the first sentence
             used to do wrongly.
           - login_tls is a connection that was established and whose secure transport then failed.
           - login_unreadable_body is the case where the server answered and the reply could not be read
             as this client's envelope. The server was reached; only its answer was unusable.
         All three close with "Tente de novo", the same verb the refusals above use, so the operator reads
         one voice rather than three. -->
    <string name="login_timeout">O servidor não respondeu a tempo. Tente de novo.</string>
    <string name="login_tls">Falha na conexão segura com o servidor. Tente de novo.</string>
    <string name="login_unreadable_body">Resposta do servidor não reconhecida. Tente de novo.</string>
    <!-- The residue's sentence: the cause the taxonomy could not name. It says only that signing in did
         not happen and invites a retry, and it names no cause at all, not the network, because an
         unclassified Throwable has not been shown to involve one. This is the sentence `Unknown` used to
         borrow from `login_unreachable`, which sent the operator to check a network the failure had not been
         shown to involve. -->
    <string name="login_unknown">Não foi possível entrar. Tente de novo.</string>

    <string name="report_submit">Salvar relatório</string>
    <string name="report_created">Relatório %1$d salvo.</string>
    <!-- The edit surface, the web's own `ReportEditPage`. Each is that page's own `pt-BR` text for the
         same control, out of the locale's `reports` block:
           - `editTitle` is "Editar", and it is one string here because the web uses the one key for
             both jobs this app needs it for: this screen's heading, and the label of the action on the
             report detail that opens it (`ReportViewPage` draws `reports.editTitle` on its edit button).
           - `updateReport` is the save action. It is deliberately not `report_submit` above, which is
             this app's own word for the create: the two writes are different routes (that one makes a
             report "Salvar relatório", this one changes one "Atualizar Relatório") and the web names
             them differently too, with `saveReport` and `updateReport`.
           - `notesLabel` is the notes field's label. Its placeholder (`notesPlaceholder`) is not used:
             a field whose label already says what it is does not need a second sentence inside it.
         The two shift names the chips draw are the history's pair below rather than two more strings:
         they spell the same two shifts, and this file shares a word rather than writing it twice, for the
         same reason `history_previous` is the loading history's too. -->
    <string name="report_edit">Editar</string>
    <string name="report_update">Atualizar Relatório</string>
    <string name="report_notes">Observações</string>
    <string name="report_back">Voltar</string>
    <string name="report_photos">Fotos (%1$d/%2$d)</string>
    <string name="report_photo_add">Tirar foto</string>
    <string name="report_photo_remove">Remover</string>
    <!-- Shared by both export surfaces: the text is the server's WhatsApp message either way, so the
         sentence lives once and neither screen owns the other's name. -->
    <string name="export_load">Gerar texto do WhatsApp</string>
    <string name="export_copy">Copiar texto</string>

    <!-- The history of the reports this surface produces. Its own door is on the generator rather than
         in a third tab, so the action there is named for where it goes and not for the tab it is not.
         The word is shared with the loading history's own door, which makes the same choice on the
         grid for the same reason: both histories list what their surface produces, so the way in is an
         action on that surface. The two screens' filter, paging and lifecycle words are shared below
         for the same reason, since they name the same controls, while the sentences that name a report
         or a batch are each screen's own. -->
    <string name="report_history">Histórico</string>
    <string name="history_title">Histórico de relatórios</string>
    <string name="history_all_reports">Todos os relatórios</string>
    <string name="history_filtered">Relatórios de %1$s</string>
    <string name="history_filter_day">Dia</string>
    <string name="history_filter_month">Mês</string>
    <string name="history_filter_day_value">Dia: %1$s</string>
    <string name="history_filter_month_value">Mês: %1$s</string>
    <string name="history_clear_filters">Limpar</string>
    <string name="history_month_picker_title">Escolha o mês</string>
    <string name="history_turno_tarde">Tarde</string>
    <string name="history_turno_noite">Noite</string>
    <string name="history_inactive">Inativo</string>
    <string name="history_deactivate">Desativar</string>
    <string name="history_delete">Excluir</string>
    <!-- The only action on this screen that asks twice: the deletion is irreversible on the server. -->
    <string name="history_delete_confirm_title">Excluir este relatório?</string>
    <string name="history_delete_confirm_body">Não dá para desfazer: ele sai para todo mundo, não só desta tela.</string>
    <string name="history_empty">Nenhum relatório encontrado para este período.</string>
    <string name="history_pagination">Página %1$d de %2$d (%3$d registros)</string>
    <string name="history_previous">Anterior</string>
    <string name="history_next">Próxima</string>

    <!-- The loading history. One row is one batch, meaning a day's entries rather than one entry, so
         the copy that names what a row is belongs to this screen; the words shared with the report
         history above are the ones that name a control rather than a thing. -->
    <string name="loading_history_title">Histórico de carregamentos</string>
    <string name="loading_history_all">Todos os carregamentos</string>
    <string name="loading_history_filtered">Carregamentos de %1$s</string>
    <!-- Labelled, because the loading date is not the batch date above it on six days out of seven. -->
    <string name="loading_history_loading_date">Carregamento: %1$s</string>
    <string name="loading_history_loadings">%1$d carregamentos</string>
    <string name="loading_history_empty">Nenhum carregamento encontrado para este período.</string>
    <!-- The batch's own date, so the confirmation names the day the operator is about to lose. -->
    <string name="loading_history_delete_confirm_title">Excluir o carregamento de %1$s?</string>
    <string name="loading_history_delete_confirm_body">Não dá para desfazer: todos os lançamentos desse dia saem para todo mundo, não só desta tela.</string>

    <!-- The dashboard: the surface the app opens on, and the web's own `/dashboard`. Every string below
         except `dashboard_unreachable` is the web's own `pt-BR` text, character for character, out of its
         `dashboard` and `nav` blocks, because the two clients have to say the same thing about the same
         numbers. The two breakdown lines keep the web's own arrow, written rather than redrawn as an icon:
         the glyph is the locale file's text, and a picture of it would be a second thing to keep in
         agreement with the first. -->
    <string name="nav_dashboard">Dashboard</string>
    <string name="dashboard_overview">Visão Geral</string>
    <string name="dashboard_reports_today">Relatórios Hoje</string>
    <string name="dashboard_schedules_tomorrow">Carregamentos Amanhã</string>
    <string name="dashboard_active_users">Usuários Ativos</string>
    <string name="dashboard_reports_total">Relatórios Totais</string>
    <string name="dashboard_schedules_total">Histórico de Cargamento Total</string>
    <string name="dashboard_higiene_count">Hig. &amp; Org. -> %1$d Tarefas</string>
    <string name="dashboard_recepcion_count">Recepção -> %1$d Mercadorias</string>
    <string name="dashboard_deferred_notice">Novos módulos estarão disponíveis em versões futuras.</string>
    <!-- The one string here the web does not have, and it says what it says on purpose. It names no
         cause, because a timeout, a refused connection and a reply nobody could read are not
         distinguishable from this screen, and it is not the web's own "Erro ao carregar dashboard"
         because that wording would invite the operator to report a fault the read has not been shown to
         have. See the dashboard view model for why the failure is a flag rather than a sentence. -->
    <string name="dashboard_unreachable">Não foi possível carregar o resumo. Tente de novo.</string>

    <string name="nav_reports">Relatórios</string>
    <string name="nav_loading">Horários</string>

    <!-- The account. Its entry point is not a third tab: it sits at the far end of the tab row, because
         the two tabs are the app's jobs and this is who the operator is. The labels are the words the
         desktop screen uses for the same fields, since the operators read both. -->
    <string name="profile_title">Perfil</string>
    <string name="profile_account">Conta</string>
    <string name="profile_username">Usuário</string>
    <string name="profile_display_name">Nome</string>
    <string name="profile_role">Função</string>
    <!-- The two roles the database has. The contract types `role` as a bare string, so an unknown value
         is shown as it arrived rather than as one of these two. -->
    <string name="profile_role_administrator">Administrador</string>
    <string name="profile_role_worker">Trabalhador</string>
    <string name="profile_save">Salvar</string>
    <string name="profile_name_saved">Nome atualizado.</string>
    <!-- One string for the block and for the action that sends it: both name the same thing. -->
    <string name="profile_change_password">Alterar senha</string>
    <string name="profile_current_password">Senha atual</string>
    <string name="profile_new_password">Nova senha</string>
    <!-- The server's minimum, stated where the password is typed instead of discovered as a 400. The
         number is the view model's constant, so the rule and the sentence cannot drift apart. -->
    <string name="profile_password_rule">A nova senha precisa de pelo menos %1$d caracteres.</string>
    <!-- No confirmation accompanies this action: see the screen for why it does not ask twice. -->
    <string name="profile_sign_out">Sair da conta</string>
    <!-- Shown in place of the form once the session has ended. The password change brings its own
         sentence, because that sign-out is one the operator did not ask for. -->
    <string name="profile_signed_out">Sessão encerrada.</string>
    <string name="profile_back_to_login">Entrar de novo</string>
    <!-- The APK's own version, for the operator who asks whether the update reached the phone. It is read
         from the build, never typed here, so the number on screen is the number that was packaged. Kept out
         of the account block on purpose: see the screen for why it is drawn even when nothing loaded. -->
    <string name="profile_app_version">Versão do aplicativo</string>
    <!-- What the check that runs when the screen opens found, drawn under the version it is about.
         Three sentences for three voices, and the difference between them is the point: "up to date"
         is quiet because there is nothing to do; "there is a new version" is the one that wants the
         eye, because the download action is directly below it; and a check that could not be made says
         so in the same quiet voice, because it is not a fault of the operator's session. It says
         "verificar" rather than "baixar" or "atualizar" on purpose: nothing is downloaded and nothing
         is installed by this app, it only asks GitHub which release is newest. See the screen for why
         this line is not drawn in the error colour. -->
    <string name="profile_update_up_to_date">Você já está na versão mais recente.</string>
    <!-- The number is the newest release without the tag's "v", so it reads the same way the version
         line above it does. -->
    <string name="profile_update_available">Há uma versão nova: %1$s</string>
    <string name="profile_update_unavailable">Não foi possível verificar se há uma versão nova.</string>
    <!-- The way to the download, drawn next to the version it is about: the operator reads the version,
         sees it is old, and this is the tap that takes them to the page it is published on. It says
         "baixar" rather than "atualizar" on purpose: the app downloads nothing and installs nothing;
         the browser does that, which is why this app needs no install permission. The address is not here:
         it is a build declaration, because where the APK is hosted is not a sentence anyone writes
         (see RELEASES_URL in build.gradle.kts). -->
    <string name="profile_download_update">Baixar nova versão</string>
    <!-- The one thing about this action that looks like a fault the first time and is not. An APK that did
         not come from the store makes the phone ask once whether to allow it, and that prompt arrives
         seconds after this button is tapped: a screen that says nothing about it turns a normal first
         install into something that reads as gone wrong. One sentence, because it is one fact. -->
    <string name="profile_download_update_hint">Na primeira vez o telefone pergunta se pode instalar um aplicativo de fora da loja, e pergunta uma vez só.</string>
    <!-- The tap above that found nothing on this phone able to open a link, which is reachable because an
         APK also arrives by adb, by MDM or from a file manager and none of those proves a browser exists.
         It says what to do about it, since a button that silently does nothing is what support would
         otherwise have to guess at, and it does not repeat the address: the address is a build declaration
         and the log line carries it. -->
    <string name="profile_download_update_failed">Nenhum aplicativo deste telefone abre links, então a página de download não abriu. Instale um navegador e toque de novo, ou baixe o APK no computador e passe para o telefone.</string>
    <string name="loading_title">Cronograma de carregamento</string>
    <string name="loading_fleteros">%1$d/%2$d fleteros</string>
    <string name="loading_add_entry">Adicionar</string>
    <string name="loading_pick_driver">Motorista</string>
    <string name="loading_pick_vehicle">Veículo</string>
    <string name="loading_confirm">Confirmar</string>
    <string name="loading_cancel">Cancelar</string>
